#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/id #uuid "3d38f3e0-4c27-5ac6-ac16-9c8f57e0000e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4327" :question/problem "Find the angle between $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,5,-2 \\rangle$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{6} \\right) \\approx 111.9^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/id #uuid "d6d1be58-7321-50ca-a47d-5e80d55277c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4328" :question/problem "Find $\\angle BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,-5)$, $B(-1,0,-4)$, and $C(5,1,1).$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,-5⟩,\\,b=⟨-1,0,-4⟩,\\,c=⟨5,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\\angle BAC = \\theta.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "fb2df468-d70d-5300-8860-0cb729cb13c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5112" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4$, find the angle between $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle t, \\, -2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 6, \\, t \\rangle$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-4 \\\\ t\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot t & =-4 \\\\ 6t-2t & =-4 \\\\ 4t & =-4 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨-1,-2⟩,\\,b=⟨6,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{185}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{185}} \\right) \\approx 107.1^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1e08c5-8191-51cb-828c-4e1bf4d18edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269075} {:answer/id #uuid "c48d61db-8a7b-58a9-baa3-b45f0e2b2e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269076} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe43b6a-8e20-5aa9-b47c-1e0b409544b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269077} {:answer/id #uuid "a8481d0e-1847-5899-a263-24206ff622f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269078} {:answer/id #uuid "f68369eb-d5b2-597a-8ad3-4e02ef54893c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe43b6a-8e20-5aa9-b47c-1e0b409544b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4237779a-fb1d-5aae-9935-ca22c819fe5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16a09fd3-be95-51b4-b0ac-0c24039ae9ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-101511" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,1)$, $B(3,1,-2)$, and $C(-1,1,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,1,-2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{182}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{182}})≈{86}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe092e6a-a292-562a-8e10-3a18cf911c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305575} {:answer/id #uuid "9012605f-9225-5ab8-adaf-c6233a381fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305576} {:answer/id #uuid "cd96dcf2-df7b-5d7d-b63a-2f885d7a0ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305577} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c459a6-1bf4-521f-b918-6d61ed3c3273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305578} {:answer/id #uuid "5a531894-930d-5aa8-be0a-b7eeb2655660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6c459a6-1bf4-521f-b918-6d61ed3c3273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305578} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d14433-e6bf-53f2-aba4-b6c9efded31e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fe38227-7ce9-5330-a6c3-677c59d25bae" :question/math-academy-id "q-101512" :question/problem "Given that $a\\cdot b=-5$, find the angle between $a=⟨2t,t⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $a\\cdot b=-5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-5 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 3 & =-5 \\\\ 2t+3t & =-5 \\\\ 5t & =-5 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨2(-1),\\,(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={135}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b799d246-a11b-5070-8463-6dd43a31f2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{23}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305589} {:answer/id #uuid "0efd139f-2138-5c6a-8e89-f0113b4b5529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} {:answer/id #uuid "5e90994a-68d7-554e-8846-e8e826dcaf09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305591} {:answer/id #uuid "77dbf72a-adae-5231-b827-7a1763244fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305592} {:answer/id #uuid "a49f55ad-0b4a-59d1-9d05-998e90849253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0efd139f-2138-5c6a-8e89-f0113b4b5529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} :answer-field/id #uuid "45442419-9910-5c12-9801-3473e30f7e7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2da436d8-ed5e-54f6-ba9e-01977eb421f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124826" :question/problem "Find $∠XZY$ given the points $X(-2,1,0)$, $Y(-1,0,4)$, and $Z(-1,2,-2).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-2,1,0⟩,\\,y=⟨-1,0,4⟩,\\,z=⟨-1,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩-⟨-1,2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4⟩-⟨-1,2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{40}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{240}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{4\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{15}})={25}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9d024a-612a-5b88-b561-2eca5dd12c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269152} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee2a05a-ca70-5223-ba9e-36cd6fa08e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269153} {:answer/id #uuid "6eaac85c-277b-51f1-baf6-a4d8434347b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269154} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eef7ee-ce27-568c-aa25-74f1486ec107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269155} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ec3a5-7e51-5c63-9ee0-131bcdf218d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ec3a5-7e51-5c63-9ee0-131bcdf218d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269156} :answer-field/id #uuid "89db74db-3d63-59f5-b41d-544e3f33dabd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61191758-28d5-55a6-95f0-ed679272a908" :question/math-academy-id "q-77164" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(3t,t,4),$ $B(3t,4t,3)$ and $C(5t,t+1,5).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩,\\,b=⟨3t,\\,4t,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨5t,\\,t+1,\\,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3t,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5t,\\,t+1,\\,5⟩-⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2$, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =2 \\\\ 0\\cdot 2t+3t\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ 3t-1 & =2 \\\\ 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,3,-1⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}})≈{75}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
